<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW ═══════════ -->
<section class="section" id="executive-overview">
  <h2 class="section-title">Executive Overview</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">High-level summary of dataset scope and critical findings on AI's impact across the 2015–2035 horizon.</p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Dataset Scope</h4>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">{d['overview']['total_rows']:,} records spanning {d['overview']['year_range']} across {d['overview']['num_countries']} countries, {d['overview']['num_job_titles']} distinct job roles, and {d['overview']['num_skills']} primary skills. Covers Entry, Mid, and Senior experience tiers with Bachelor, Master, and PhD education levels.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Historical &amp; Projected Periods</h4>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">2015–2024 represents <strong>historical / observed</strong> data. 2025–2035 represents <strong>simulated projections</strong> based on current AI adoption trajectories and labor market modeling. All future figures should be interpreted as trend projections, not predictions.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Critical Finding 1:</strong> The average AI risk score climbs from <strong>{d['risk_trend']['2015']}</strong> in 2015 to <strong>{d['risk_trend']['2035']}</strong> in 2035 — a <strong>+{abs(round((d['risk_trend']['2035'] - d['risk_trend']['2015']) * 100, 0)):.0f}%</strong> increase. This signals a structural shift in automation exposure across nearly all roles and geographies. No job category is fully immune, though the pace of risk escalation varies significantly by role type.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-red">
    <strong>Critical Finding 2:</strong> There is a <strong>strong negative correlation (r = {d['highlights']['salary_risk_correlation']})</strong> between median salary and AI risk. Higher-paying, specialized roles — particularly those requiring advanced degrees and technical mastery — carry substantially lower automation risk. Conversely, lower-salary roles in more routine-oriented functions face the highest displacement threat. This creates a <strong>compounding inequality risk</strong>: AI disruption disproportionately threatens lower-income workers.
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-green">
    <strong>Critical Finding 3:</strong> Overall job survival stands at <strong>{d['highlights']['overall_survival_rate']}%</strong> when counting roles classified as moderate (class 1) or strong (class 2) survival. However, this aggregate masks significant variation. By 2035, roles requiring {d['highlights']['fastest_skill']} expertise, advanced degrees, and located in high-investment markets show the strongest resilience profiles.
  </div>

  <div class="card-grid" style="margin-top:20px;">
